Disaster-related trauma
Social & Environmental
Flashbacks/intrusive memories, Acute physical injuries, Exacerbation of chronic diseases

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-028
Reason for Visit: Acute severe respiratory distress and acute stress reaction following a catastrophic residential fire/environmental disaster.
Location: Emergency evacuation center
Present at Visit: Patient, Red Cross volunteer
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an elderly patient who had just been evacuated from a severe wildfire zone, presenting with acute shortness of breath, severe chest tightness, and profound shock. Urgent assessment was required to treat acute smoke inhalation, reverse bronchospasm, and manage disaster-related trauma prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My chest is burning, I can't stop coughing up black stuff, and I just watched my house burn to the ground."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 4-hour history of severe, progressive dyspnea, painful coughing, and throat irritation immediately following evacuation from a catastrophic wildfire. Key symptoms include acute respiratory exacerbations from the inhalation of smoke and toxic debris. The patient exhibits an acute onset of severe anxiety, hyperarousal, and emotional numbing, describing intrusive, distressing memories of the fire front. There is an exacerbation of pre-existing chronic diseases (COPD and hypertension) due to the extreme physiological stress and the abrupt loss of all maintenance medications in the fire. The patient is experiencing a profound grief reaction and acute displacement, currently residing in an overcrowded shelter.
Functional Status: Independent but acutely incapacitated by respiratory distress and psychological shock
Social/Safety Concerns: Total loss of property and medications, acute smoke inhalation injury, high risk of PTSD development

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 175/100 | HR: 115 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 28 | SpO2: 90% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Soot-stained face and clothing, smelling strongly of smoke, coughing continuously (producing carbonaceous sputum), visibly trembling, tearful
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals widespread, tight expiratory wheezes, harsh upper airway transmitted sounds, and a markedly prolonged expiratory phase. The oropharynx is erythematous with visible soot, but no frank stridor or blistering is noted. Cardiovascular exam shows sinus tachycardia. Diagnostic tetrad features are starkly present: active flashbacks/intrusive memories of the disaster, acute physical injuries (smoke inhalation/airway burns), and exacerbation of chronic diseases (COPD).
Environmental Observations: Evacuation center is chaotic, loud, and smells of smoke; poor air quality even indoors.
Medication Reconciliation: All regular medications (inhalers, antihypertensives) destroyed in the fire.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Disaster-related trauma (Acute smoke inhalation & COPD exacerbation / Acute Stress Reaction) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Carbon Monoxide Poisoning (severe) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The patient is fully conscious, alert, and lacks the classic "cherry red" skin or profound neurological depression, though mild CO exposure is certain.
2. Acute Thermal Airway Burn (requiring intubation) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of stridor, hoarse voice, or visible blistering in the oropharynx, though the airway is severely irritated.
3. Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The respiratory distress is entirely consistent with the massive toxic inhalation and bronchospasm, rather than isolated cardiac ischemia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, life-threatening respiratory compromise secondary to toxic smoke inhalation and a severe exacerbation of underlying COPD, occurring simultaneously with an acute, catastrophic psychological trauma. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in rapid respiratory failure due to untreated bronchospasm and airway edema. The objective findings of hypoxia (SpO2 90%), severe wheeze, and carbonaceous sputum completely justify the urgent after-hours MBS intervention to initiate immediate, aggressive bronchodilator and oxygen therapy, and to coordinate emergency hospital transfer for observation of delayed airway compromise.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate administration of high-flow oxygen via a non-rebreather mask. Continuous nebulized salbutamol and ipratropium bromide. Administration of oral corticosteroids (Prednisolone).
Positive Behavioral Change: By keeping the oxygen mask firmly on your face even when you feel panicky, you ensure your brain and heart get the oxygen they are starving for, which will immediately reduce the feeling of suffocating and calm your racing heart.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Immediate referral to disaster relief social workers for emergency accommodation, clothing, and complete medication replacement.
DME/Safety (Optional): None
Follow-up: Handover to the Emergency Department team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate priority transport to the nearest Emergency Department via ambulance. Smoke inhalation injuries can cause delayed, fatal swelling of the airway hours after the event; the patient must be observed in a hospital setting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute breathing difficulty will improve with the nebulizers, but you must be monitored in the hospital because smoke damage can worsen suddenly. The psychological shock is a normal reaction to an abnormal, catastrophic event.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not take the oxygen mask off. The ambulance is on its way. Let the nurses at the shelter pack a bag of basic supplies for you.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The toxic smoke has severely irritated and narrowed your lung tubes. The oxygen and the medication in the mask will force them open and wash out the soot. You must go to the hospital to ensure your throat does not swell shut later tonight.
Actionable Steps: Focus only on taking slow, deep breaths through the mask. We have informed the social workers about your lost medications; they will organize new prescriptions for you while you are in the hospital.

